-- title:
--   Morphisms fixing k-point vanishing preserve the zero-scheme support
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field, let $X$ be a scheme, and let $f : X \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be a morphism that is locally of finite type. Let $M$ be a sheaf of modules on $X$ which is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit module $\mathcal O_U$, let $s : \mathbf 1 \to M$ be a morphism from the unit object, i.e. a global section of $M$, and let $T : X \to X$ be any morphism of schemes. Write $Z \subseteq X$ for the support of `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal s`, the smallest ideal sheaf datum on $X$ whose ideal on each affine open $U$ contains the ideal generated by the coefficients of $s$ on $U$. Assume that for every morphism $z : \operatorname{Spec} k \to X$ with $z$ followed by $f$ equal to the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} k$, the pullback of $s$ along $z$ vanishes if and only if the pullback of $s$ along $z$ followed by $T$ vanishes. Then the preimage of $Z$ under the underlying continuous map of $T$ equals $Z$ as subsets of $X$.
--
--   This is the statement that a self-morphism of $X$ preserving the vanishing locus of a section on $k$-rational points preserves the whole support of its zero scheme, the passage from $k$-points to all scheme points being supplied by Jacobson-ness of schemes locally of finite type over a field. It is used in the study of polarisations, where it feeds the finiteness statement [`AlgebraicGeometry.Polarisation.finite_setOf_forall_pullbackSection_eq_zero_iff_of_finite_kernelPts`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.Polarisation.finite_setOf_forall_pullbackSection_eq_zero_iff_of_finite_kernelPts).
